Q: Asynchronous Python+webview Issues When File Creation Is Involved A: <p>It looks like <strong>pywebview doesn’t support async functions in the Python API</strong>. Any methods exposed through <code>js_api</code> need to be synchronous. Using <code>async def</code>, <code>await</code>, or <code>asyncio.run()</code> in these methods doesn’t actually make them asynchronous and can end up breaking the JavaScript callback handling.</p>
<p>If you need to perform long-running or blocking work (such as file I/O or CPU-heavy processing), that work should be moved to a background thread, while the API method itself returns immediately.</p>
<p>Additionally, in the HTML, a <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> inside a <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> will reload the page by default. That page reload breaks the pywebview context and cancels any pending JS callbacks, so you need to prevent the form from submitting or use a button that doesn’t trigger a reload.</p>
<p>contained_input.html</p>
<pre><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
  &lt;head&gt;
    &lt;meta charset=&quot;UTF-8&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;Code Generator&lt;/title&gt;

    &lt;style&gt;
      * {
        font-family: Arial;
        font-size: 16px;
        padding: 0;
        margin: 0;
      }

      #copyCount {
        width: 10%;
        text-align: right;
      }

      button {
        display: block;
        margin-left: auto;
        margin-right: auto;
        padding: 3px 10px;
      }

      table {
        padding-left: 5%;
        padding-right: 5%;
        width: 100%;
      }

      table,
      th,
      td {
        border: 1px solid black;
      }
    &lt;/style&gt;
  &lt;/head&gt;

  &lt;body&gt;
    &lt;h1&gt;Code Generator&lt;/h1&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;

    &lt;!-- Ensuring that the page does not reload --&gt;
    &lt;form id=&quot;settingsForm&quot; onsubmit=&quot;makeRequest(); return false;&quot;&gt;
      &lt;div&gt;
        &lt;label for=&quot;copyCount&quot;&gt;
          Enter Desired Number Of Codes (rounded to the nearest even number):
        &lt;/label&gt;
        &lt;input
          id=&quot;copyCount&quot;
          type=&quot;number&quot;
          value=&quot;2&quot;
          min=&quot;2&quot;
          max=&quot;100&quot;
          step=&quot;2&quot;
          required
        /&gt;
      &lt;/div&gt;

      &lt;br /&gt;

      &lt;div&gt;
        &lt;label for=&quot;amount&quot;&gt;Select The Amount:&lt;/label&gt;
        &lt;select id=&quot;amount&quot;&gt;
          &lt;option value=&quot;5&quot;&gt;5&lt;/option&gt;
          &lt;option value=&quot;10&quot;&gt;10&lt;/option&gt;
          &lt;option value=&quot;15&quot;&gt;15&lt;/option&gt;
        &lt;/select&gt;
      &lt;/div&gt;

      &lt;br /&gt;

      &lt;button type=&quot;submit&quot;&gt;Submit&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/form&gt;

    &lt;br /&gt;

    &lt;p id=&quot;result&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;script&gt;
      function submitForm(response) {
        document.getElementById(&quot;result&quot;).innerText = response;
      }

      async function makeRequest() {
        const copyCount = Number(
          document.getElementById(&quot;copyCount&quot;).value
        );
        const amount = document.getElementById(&quot;amount&quot;).value;

        // Prevents program from running if copyCount is odd
        if (copyCount % 2 !== 0) {
          return;
        }

        try {
          const output =
            await pywebview.api.submit_generation_request([
              copyCount,
              amount,
            ]);

          submitForm(output);
        } catch (error) {
          console.error(error);
        }
      }
    &lt;/script&gt;
  &lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
</code></pre>
